## 1. Findings — how OpenChamber mobile connects to OpenChamber desktop

### 1.1 Topology (the single most important fact)

In OpenChamber, **the desktop app *is* the server**. The Electron main process imports and starts the
web server **in-process**:

```js
// desktop-asar/dist-bundle/main.mjs (esbuild bundle; banner strings retained)
const { startWebUiServer } = await import("@openchamber/web/server/index.js");
const handle = await startWebUiServer({ port: chosenPort, host: bindHost,
  uiPassword: desktopUiPassword || null, attachSignals: false, exitOnShutdown: false, ... });
```

- Server package: `@openchamber/web` (`main: ./server/index.js`, `startWebUiServer`).
- The desktop UI, the browser UI, and the phone all talk to **one origin** served by that process.
- Bind host defaults to `127.0.0.1`; a LAN/pairing URL is supplied explicitly to the pairing
  payload (see §1.3), and workspace remotes default `0.0.0.0` only with a UI password.
- The Electron shell is also a first-class client: it creates a local device token with
  `clientKind: "desktop-local"` (`LOCAL_DESKTOP_CLIENT_KIND`) and treats it as the trusted operator
  of the server. Remote desktops are `clientKind: "desktop"`.

The **mobile app** is a Capacitor WebView (`com.openchamber.app`) that bundles a copy of the web UI's
`mobile.html`/`MobileApp` entry and talks to whichever server it paired with (a desktop instance, a
headless install, or the same server via relay). The Android shell config:

```json
// AndroidManifest-adjacent: assets/capacitor.config.json
{ "appId": "com.openchamber.app", "appName": "OpenChamber",
  "server": { "androidScheme": "https" }, "android": { "allowMixedContent": true }, ... }
```

Plugins registered: secure-storage (`@aparajita/capacitor-secure-storage`), MLKit barcode scanning,
app, keyboard, push-notifications, status-bar.

### 1.2 The connection model: unified "pairing v2" with transport candidates

The heart of the design is that a connection is **not** a single URL. It is a set of **transport
candidates**, and the client races/probes them and keeps working across address changes.

Candidate shapes (server `core-routes.js`, mobile `Ib`/`Hf`):

```
{ type: "lan",    url: "http://<host>:<port>", priority: 10 }     // plain HTTP on LAN/Tailnet
{ type: "tunnel", url: "https://<public-host>", priority: 10/20 } // reverse-proxy / ngrok / cloudflare
{ type: "relay",  relayUrl: "wss://…", serverId: "<id>",
                  hostEncPubJwk: { kty:"EC", crv:"P-256", x, y }, priority: 30 }
```

- Direct (`lan`/`tunnel`) candidates are plain HTTP(S). The mobile client normalizes both to
  internal `kind: "direct"`; the relay candidate stays `kind: "relay"`.
- The **relay** candidate carries **no token** — it is a transport, not a link format. The one-time
  pairing secret is redeemed *over* the relay like over any other candidate.
- Priority is a numeric hint (direct ~10/20, relay 30): direct is preferred, relay is the fallback
  when the host is not directly reachable (NAT, no public URL).

### 1.3 Pairing flow (desktop creates the link, phone scans it)

**Server endpoints** (all in `server/lib/opencode/core-routes.js`, behind the `/api` auth guard):

| Method + path | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `POST /api/client-auth/pairing/sessions` | Create a one-time pairing session. Body: `{ label?, allowedClientKinds?, serverUrl?, includeDirect?, includeRelay? }`. Returns `{ pairing: { id, secret, expiresAt, fingerprint, … }, server: { label, candidates } }` |
| `GET /api/client-auth/pairing/transports` | Current direct transports for the create-device dialog → `{ local, lan, relayAvailable }` |
| `GET /api/client-auth/pairing/sessions` | Pending (unredeemed) sessions, secrets excluded |
| `DELETE /api/client-auth/pairing/sessions/:id` | Cancel a pending session |
| `POST /api/client-auth/pairing/redeem` | **Unauthenticated** (rate-limited): body `{ pairingId, secret, clientLabel?, clientKind?, deviceName?, devicePlatform?, deviceModel?, appVersion?, dedupeKey? }` → `{ ok, server:{ label, url, fingerprint }, client, clientToken }` |
| `GET /api/client-auth/connection/candidates` | **Candidate refresh for an already-paired device** (UI session or client bearer): returns the server's *current* LAN URLs (+ relay when enabled) and `serverId` |
| `GET/POST/DELETE /api/client-auth/clients[/:id]` | List / create / revoke paired devices (token minting) |

**Pairing-session internals** (`server/lib/client-auth/pairing.js`):

- id prefix `pair_`, secret = 32 random bytes base64url, TTL **10 min**, stored **hashed**
  (sha256) in `client-pairing-sessions.json`; `constantTimeEqual` on redeem; generic
  `"Invalid or expired pairing session"` on any failure; per-IP+pairingId rate limit on redeem.
- Operator-typed `label` wins; device self-reported name is the fallback; `dedupeKey` makes a
  re-pair replace the same device record instead of duplicating.
- On redeem it calls `remoteClientAuthRuntime.createClient(...)` and returns the raw token once.

**Device tokens** (`server/lib/client-auth/remote-clients.js`):

- Format **`oc_client_<32 random bytes base64url>`**, stored **hashed** in `remote-clients.json`.
- **Non-expiring**, revocable; last-used timestamp written at most once/minute.
- Conveyed as `Authorization: Bearer oc_client_…`.
- WebSocket upgrades can't carry headers, so they authenticate with a **short-lived URL-scoped
  token** minted beforehand (relay doc, §1.5).

**The QR / deep-link payload** (the contract between desktop and phone):

```
openchamber://connect?v=2&p=<base64url(JSON)>
JSON = {
  v: 2,
  pairingId: "pair_…",
  secret: "…",
  label?: "<server label>",
  fingerprint?: "A1B2-C3D4",
  expiresAt?: "<ISO>",
  candidates: [ …the candidate list above… ]
}
```

- Built desktop-side by the shared UI helper (`openchamber://connect?...` builder + zod schema
  `Vc`), **not** returned by the server route — v2.3.3's `pairing/sessions` response returns
  `server.candidates` and the UI assembles the URI. (Vela Mobile's v1.20 fork differs slightly:
  its server *does* return a prebuilt `vela://connect?...` `connectionUrl`, see §2.2.)
- Mobile parsing: `new URL(...)`, requires protocol `openchamber:`, host `connect`, `v=2`, then
  base64url-decodes `p`, JSON-parses, zod-validates, drops the payload if expired. A lenient
  fallback parser tolerates a manually-entered `openchamber://connect?...` string (QR fallback).
- `fingerprint` is a human-checkable `XXXX-XXXX` hex shown on both ends to confirm the right host.

### 1.4 Runtime connect algorithm (phone side)

Recovered from the mobile bundle (`renderMobileApp-*.js`):

1. **Probe direct candidates first.** For each `kind:"direct"` URL: `GET {url}/health`; if a pinned
   `serverId` exists, read `health.serverId` and **reject a mismatch before sending the token**;
   then `GET {url}/auth/session` with `Authorization: Bearer <clientToken>` and
   `credentials: "omit"`; classify `ok | needs-login | auth-failed`.
2. **Fall back to relay** when direct is unreachable: open the E2EE tunnel
   (`Ko({ relayUrl, serverId, hostEncPubJwk })`), then run the same `/auth/session` through it.
3. Store the winning connection. Saved connections live in **localStorage**
   (`openchamber.mobile.connections.v1`) as `[{ id, label, candidates, hasToken|clientToken,
   lastUsedAt }]`; the raw token is kept in **Capacitor secure storage** on native (the record only
   stores `hasToken`), so plain localStorage never holds a secret on device.
4. A stable `deviceId` (`openchamber.mobile.deviceId`, `crypto.randomUUID`) yields the
   `dedupeKey` (`mobile:<deviceId>`), stabilising re-pairing.
5. Already-paired clients periodically call `GET /api/client-auth/connection/candidates` to refresh
   stale LAN addresses (DHCP change) and stay off the relay when direct works.

### 1.5 The relay (E2EE) — "reachable from anywhere" without exposing the Mac

From `server/lib/relay/` + `packages/ui/src/lib/relay/` (identical architecture in Vela Mobile's
v1.20 fork, documented in `vela-mobile/packages/web/server/lib/relay/DOCUMENTATION.md`):

- **Layer 1 — routing.** The host dials **outbound** to the relay (`wss://relay.openchamber.dev/ws`
  by default). Nothing inbound is exposed. Host authenticates with a long-lived signing key; the
  relay routes each client to the host by a stable `serverId`
  (base64url sha256 of the public signing JWK).
- **Layer 2 — E2EE.** Client↔host ECDH (P-256) + HKDF (`openchamber-relay-v1`) → AES-GCM frames
  (`[1 byte version][12 byte IV][ciphertext+16 byte tag]`). The relay forwards **opaque ciphertext**
  and cannot read app traffic. The host's encryption public key is the client's trust anchor,
  distributed out-of-band in the pairing payload (`hostEncPubJwk`).
- **Layer 3 — multiplexing.** A tunnel codec frames HTTP request/response (incl. streamed bodies),
  **SSE** (streamed HTTP), and **WebSocket** sub-streams (`TunnelFrameType.HttpRequest/HttpBody/
  HttpResponse/StreamEnd/WsOpen/…/Ping/Pong/DeliveryAck`), so the *entire* app works over the one
  encrypted connection.
- **Auth is unchanged by the relay:** the tunnel is transport only; the server authenticates every
  tunneled request exactly as a direct remote client.
- Relay is **opt-in, off by default**; a per-machine host claim (`relay-host.lock`) picks which
  local process owns the single relay host slot. `serverId`/`hostEncPubJwk` persist in
  `settings.json` (`privateRelay`) and are never rotated implicitly.
- Two byte-compatible implementations (TS client in `packages/ui`, JS host in `@openchamber/web`)
  are held together by a cross-compat test — this is the "do not let them drift" invariant.

### 1.6 Push notifications

- Native iOS = **APNs**; Android = **FCM** (upstream); registered to the *connected server*, which
  forwards through a central push relay (`https://api.openchamber.dev/v1/push/send`) using the
  server's own ECDSA P-256 key to bind tokens to that server. Mobile payloads carry **generic text
  only** (scenario title + session name).
- **Presence-aware suppression:** a device's push is skipped while an interactive (desktop/web)
  client reports it is focused — gated on the *desktop's* visibility, never the phone's own.

### 1.7 Persistence / secrets (server side)

`$OPENCHAMBER_DATA_DIR` (default `~/.config/openchamber`) owns: `settings.json` (incl.
`privateRelay`), `remote-clients.json` (hashed device tokens), `client-pairing-sessions.json`,
signing/encryption key material, `relay-host.lock`, push token registries. Writes are atomic
(tmp+rename) and files are `0600`.

### 1.8 One-paragraph summary of the contract

> Desktop runs one web server (in-process for Electron) that is the single origin for desktop UI and
> phones. To onboard a phone, the desktop mints a 10-minute, single-use pairing session, gathers its
> reachable transport *candidates* (LAN/Tunnel direct + optional E2EE relay), and encodes
> `{pairingId, secret, candidates, …}` into `openchamber://connect?v=2&p=…` for a QR/deep link. The
> phone parses it, races the direct candidates (`/health` + `/auth/session`), falls back to the E2EE
> relay tunnel if needed, redeems the secret via `POST /api/client-auth/pairing/redeem`, and stores
> a **non-expiring `oc_client_…` bearer token** in device secure storage. Thereafter it talks to the
> one origin (direct or through the E2EE tunnel) with that bearer token, refreshes candidates when
> its LAN address goes stale, and receives push through the server's push relay.

