## 2. Analysis — the current Vela mobile app

### 2.1 What exists today

- **`vela-mobile`** is a **fork of OpenChamber v1.20.0** (`packages/web` + `packages/ui` both say
  `1.20.0`; the mobile shell is `1.13.2`; the workspace root is `0.4.0`), rebranded to Vela
  (`com.vela.mobile`, scheme `vela://`, env prefix `VELA_`, scopes `@vela/*`). Git remote:
  `Tsohnle95/VelaMobile`.
- It is **not** a thin wrapper. It is a large workspace: Capacitor shell (`packages/mobile`),
  Vite web app + Express server (`packages/web`), shared UI (`packages/ui`). Built Android APKs
  already exist in `dist/` (`vela-mobile-0.2.0 … 0.4.0.apk`).
- **It already carries the OpenChamber connection stack** from v1.20: pairing-v2
  (`vela://connect?v=2&p=…`), transport candidates (`lan`/`tunnel`/`relay`), the full E2EE relay,
  tunnels (ngrok/cloudflare providers), the `connection/candidates` refresh endpoint, and
  notifications/APNs push. Local additions on top: `vela-sessions`, `vela-control`, `session-*`
  subsystems, and `scripts/desktop-service.mjs` / `scripts/service.mjs`.
- **The mobile client logic is the same family** as upstream: `packages/ui/src/lib/connectionPayload.ts`
  parses `vela://connect`, `mobileConnections` stores candidates + token, `runtime-*` routes traffic
  through direct or relay transport.

### 2.2 How Vela Desktop integrates it (the fragile part)

`vela/src/main/mobile-server.ts` + `src/main/index.ts`:

- Vela Desktop does **not** embed a server. It **spawns a child Node process** running
  `vela-mobile/scripts/desktop-service.mjs --parent-pid <vela pid>`.
- It **discovers an on-disk checkout**: `../vela-mobile`, `~/code-repositories/vela-mobile`,
  `~/coding-projects/vela-mobile`, or `VELA_MOBILE_HOME`; and it needs a **system Node runtime**
  (`~/.local/bin/node`, Homebrew, `which node`, or `VELA_NODE_BIN`). Missing either ⇒
  `state: "setup-required"`.
- It reads `~/Library/Application Support/VelaMobile/run/desktop-status.json` for `{port,password}`,
  authenticates to the companion's `/auth/session`, then proxies the desktop's shared OpenCode
  daemon into it via `VELA_EXTERNAL_BACKEND=1` + `OPENCODE_HOST/USERNAME/PASSWORD` so phone and
  desktop **share sessions**.
- `createPairingQr()` calls the companion's `pairing/transports` + `pairing/sessions` and returns
  the prebuilt `connectionUrl`; only the URI crosses IPC
  (`shell:mobile-setup-status`, `shell:mobile-pairing-qr`) — the password/token never reach the
  renderer. That trust boundary matches the intent of `.agent/tasks/mobile-connection-onboarding.md`.
- Vela Desktop's **mobile server lifecycle is tied to the desktop app** (parent-pid watch): "app
  open = mobile works; app closed = it stops". Same intent as upstream, different mechanism.

### 2.3 Strengths of the current state

- The **protocol already exists and is mature** (pairing-v2, candidates, E2EE relay, push). This is
  not a greenfield problem.
- The **trust boundary is respected** (main process brokers pairing; renderer never sees secrets).
- A **release-signed Android path exists** (`omni-release.jks`, `dist:android`), and the workspace
  has a `verify` gate (docs/type-check/lint/test/build), even if the mobile app is not CI-built.
- Session sharing with the desktop is already designed (`VELA_EXTERNAL_BACKEND`).

### 2.4 Weaknesses / risks (in priority order)

1. **Not shippable to end users.** Requires a developer checkout of `vela-mobile` *and* a system
   Node. A packaged `Vela.app` on a normal Mac will show `setup-required`. This is the single
   biggest reliability gap. (Options A/B/C in §3 address it.)
2. **Separate process tree + version skew.** The desktop and its mobile server are two processes
   from two repos at two upstream versions (Vela fork v1.20 vs upstream v2.3.3). Every upstream
   security/connection fix must be hand-ported; drift is guaranteed.
3. **Large fork to own.** ~40 server subsystems + a full shared UI is a lot of surface to maintain
   and keep building as the toolchain moves.
4. **Push is effectively off.** No committed `google-services.json` ⇒ FCM disabled on Android
   (README states it is intentionally gated). iOS push requires a real APNs/prod setup.
5. **No native release automation.** HANDOFF.md is explicit: no CI builds/signs/publishes Android or
   iOS; TestFlight/Play internal and store metadata are unstarted. The CI gate is JS-only on Linux.
6. **Verification is thin for the actual experience.** The live audit (`scripts/e2e-audit.mjs`) and
   a device route are manual; there is no automated mobile end-to-end.

