## 6. DECISION (2026-10-16) — adopt OpenChamber v2.3.3 as the base; retire the fork

**Decision.** Use upstream **OpenChamber v2.3.3** as the starting point for the Vela/Vela mobile app.
Stop investing in the `vela-mobile` v1.20 fork as the primary base.

**Reachability decision:** **Tailscale** (already installed on Mac + phone). Relay stays **off**; the
phone reaches the Mac's tailnet address. Orthogonal to the base choice.

### Why (evidence)
- `vela-mobile` has **no upstream remote or history** (first commit = OpenChamber v1.20.0 import;
  55 commits, one author). It can never `git rebase` upstream — every fix is hand-ported forever.
- Upstream is **public, MIT, active** (11.7k★, ~5k commits), and **already on `@opencode/client 2.0.29`**
  (OpenCode v2), so much of Vela's v2-compat delta may be unnecessary.
- Upstream already solves the things the fork struggles with: **in-process Electron server**, matured
  pairing/candidates/relay, tunnels, push, VS Code/Web surfaces, release tooling.
- Vela's real deltas are **small and re-appliable**: branding (Vela/Vela, app id, scheme, env
  prefix), desktop integration, and a few local subsystems.

### The target architecture (what "connect to Vela Desktop" means)
The mobile app connects to a **server**, not to a desktop app. OpenChamber Desktop is merely that
server's host (it runs `@openchamber/web` in-process). Therefore, for Vela Mobile to pair with
**Vela Desktop** exactly like upstream pairs with OpenChamber Desktop, **Vela Desktop must become
the host of the same server**. Vela already hosts a server today — it spawns the `vela-mobile`
v1.20 companion and shares its OpenCode daemon via `VELA_EXTERNAL_BACKEND`. This is an **upgrade of
Vela Desktop's existing server role**, not a rewrite.

### Base to pin
- Repo: `github.com/openchamber/openchamber` @ tag **`v2.3.3`** (`main` is also 2.3.3).
- **Adopt:** `packages/web` (Express server — the thing the phone pairs with), `packages/mobile`
  (Capacitor shell), `packages/ui` (mobile UI), `packages/sdk` as needed.
- **Do NOT adopt:** `packages/electron` (OpenChamber Desktop). Vela Desktop keeps its own UI/runtime.
- Toolchain: **bun 1.4.3**, **Node ≥24.14**, **TypeScript ~7**, React 19, Vite 7, Express 5.

### Approach
1. **Fork upstream into a fresh repo** with `upstream` as a real remote; never mutate history.
   Track `v2.3.3`; keep Vela deltas on top as thin, rebasable commits.
2. **Rebrand** (mechanical, broad): app id / bundle id, display name (Vela/Vela), URL scheme
   (`vela://` — payload stays `?v=2&p=<base64url>`), env prefix (`VELA_*`), icons/splash, and the
   `*.openchamber.dev` endpoints (relay/push/updater) if we don't self-host.
3. **Embed the server in Vela Desktop** — run the rebranded `@openchamber/web` **in-process inside
   Vela's Electron main** (like OpenChamber Desktop), pointed at Vela's OpenCode daemon
   (`OPENCODE_HOST` / external-backend). This replaces the external `vela-mobile` companion spawn in
   `src/main/mobile-server.ts`; the pairing IPC contract (`shell:mobile-setup-status`,
   `shell:mobile-pairing-qr`) can stay. Result: no external checkout, no system-Node dependency.
4. **Port only what upstream lacks** (Vela branding, any vela-* subsystem still wanted). Upstream
   already covers most (Session Goals, folders, etc.).
5. **Tailscale reachability** + relay off.
6. **Release** — Android build/sign; iOS later; attribution (MIT LICENSE + NOTICE preserved).

### De-risk before committing (bounded spike)
- **Rebrand map**: enumerate every OpenChamber-identifying string/id/asset in `mobile`+`ui`+`web`.
- **Build spike**: build the rebranded mobile app + server, connect it to Vela's opencode backend.
- **Pairing over Tailscale**: confirm the pairing candidate carries the tailnet address, or fall back
  to entering `http://<tailnet-ip>:<port>` manually.
- **Delta audit**: confirm which Vela-only features (vela-sessions, vela-control, Vela) upstream
  already covers (Session Goals, session folders, etc.) before porting anything.

### Risks
- Large monorepo + newer toolchain (Node 24, bun 1.4.3, TS 7) vs the fork's.
- Broad-but-mechanical rebrand; easy to miss an id/asset.
- Divergence creep — the whole point is to keep deltas thin so `upstream` stays rebasable.
- MIT attribution obligations must travel with the shipped app.

